#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048189 :topic/_next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048332 :topic/math-academy-id 1159 :topic/title "Calculating Definite Integrals Using Substitution"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050071 :topic/math-academy-id 209 :topic/title "Evaluating Expressions Containing Inverse Trigonometric Functions"}] :topic/id #uuid "86b8f7ea-5908-5137-a377-052e6fc4f731" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064218 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064090 :question/id #uuid "48ec6930-88b7-5aad-9bb7-c5e23e766f80" :question/math-academy-id "e-470" :question/problem "Calculate the integral $\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{9}{4\\sqrt{1-9x^2}} \\, \\textrm{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "This integral resembles the basic integral for inverse sine,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-u^2}}\\textrm{d}u = \\arcsin(u)+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u^2$ as the variable, we have $9x^2 = (3x)^2.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=3x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}=3\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac 1 3 \\,\\textrm d u=\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nWe can now write the integral in terms of $u,$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{9}{4\\sqrt{1-9{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{9}{4}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(3x)^{2}}}\\,\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{4}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{12}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}\\cdot \\operatorname{arcsin}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡3x+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Indefinite Integrals Using Substitution with Inverse Sine"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064219 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064091 :question/id #uuid "3e89a278-54ab-59a6-adb6-38f43c0fbe9a" :question/math-academy-id "e-471" :question/problem "Calculate the integral  $\\displaystyle{\\int \\dfrac{3}{2 + 50x^2} \\, \\textrm{d}x }.$" :question/worked-solution "Note that we can rewrite this integral as\n\n$$\n\\int \\dfrac{3}{2 + 50x^2} \\, \\textrm{d}x = \\int \\dfrac{3}{2(1 + 25x^2)} = \\dfrac 3 2 \\int \\dfrac{1}{1+25x^2}\\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for inverse tangent,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+u^2}\\textrm{d}u =\\arctan u +C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u^2$ as the variable, we have $25x^2 = (5x)^2.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=5x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}=5\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac 1 5\\,\\textrm d u = \\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nWe can now write the integral in terms of $u,$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3}{2+50{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{3}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{1+25{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{1+(5x)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\,\\,\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{10}\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{10}\\cdot \\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{10}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡5x+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Indefinite Integrals Using Substitution with Inverse Tangent"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064220 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064092 :question/id #uuid "cb771ff3-b5a9-5900-913f-df23957760b1" :question/math-academy-id "e-472" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral $\\displaystyle{\\int_{0}^{\\sqrt 2} \\dfrac{1}{2+x^2}\\, \\textrm{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}\\frac{1}{2+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}\\frac{1}{2(1+{x}^{2}/2)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}\\frac{1}{2(1+(x/\\sqrt{2})^{2})}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}\\frac{1}{1+(x/\\sqrt{2})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that this integral now resembles the basic integral for the inverse tangent,\n\n$$\n\\int\\dfrac{1}{1+u^2}\\,\\textrm d u = \\arctan{u} + C.\n$$\n\nWe make the substitution $u=\\dfrac{x}{\\sqrt 2}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt 2}\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\sqrt 2 \\,\\textrm d u = \\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nBefore substituting, we use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u.$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\sqrt2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can now write the integral in terms of $u,$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}\\frac{1}{2+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\,\\cdot \\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡1-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡0) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi \\sqrt{2}}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Definite Integrals Using Substitution with Inverse Trigonometric Functions"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064250 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064101 :question/id #uuid "56860611-869d-5035-bf8f-f568c774d78d" :question/math-academy-id "e-5014" :question/problem "${\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{5\\, \\textrm{d}x}{|5x|\\sqrt{(5x)^2-1}}  = }$" :question/worked-solution "This resembles the basic integral for $\\textrm{arcsec},$\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{u^2-1}} \\textrm{d}u = \\textrm{arcsec}(u)+C.\n$$\n\nLet $u = 5x.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}=5\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n5\\,\\textrm{d}x = \\textrm{d}u.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{5\\,\\text{d}x}{|5x|\\sqrt{(5x)^{2}-1}} & =\\int \\frac{\\text{d}u}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}} \\\\  & =\\text{arcsec}(u)+C \\\\  & =\\text{arcsec}(5x)+C.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Indefinite Integrals Using Substitution with Inverse Secant"}] :topic/math-academy-id 315 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048167 :topic/math-academy-id 444 :topic/title "Integrating Functions by Completing the Square"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048503 :topic/math-academy-id 447 :topic/title "Integrating Rational Functions with Irreducible Quadratic Factors"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048618 :topic/math-academy-id 4005 :topic/title "Improper Integrals Involving Arctangent"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049562 :topic/math-academy-id 3179 :topic/title "Solving First-Order ODEs by Substitution"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050068 :topic/math-academy-id 3258 :topic/title "Integration by Substitution With Inverse Hyperbolic Functions"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050207 :topic/math-academy-id 1992 :topic/title "Double Integrals Over Rectangular Domains"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050217 :topic/math-academy-id 760 :topic/title "The Arc Length of a Planar Curve"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050379 :topic/math-academy-id 759 :topic/title "Improper Integrals of the Second Kind"}] :topic/title "Integration by Substitution With Inverse Trigonometric Functions"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048332 :topic/_next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050771 :topic/math-academy-id 443 :topic/title "Integration Using Substitution"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050819 :topic/math-academy-id 1685 :topic/title "The Sum and Constant Multiple Rules for Definite Integrals"}] :topic/id #uuid "95c6ac75-ad8e-5eab-9a14-9a2372fa3af7" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100874 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100704 :question/id #uuid "2359455c-4a44-5794-8b86-738bcdbbd11d" :question/math-academy-id "e-455" :question/problem "Calculate the definite integral $\\displaystyle\\int_{-1}^0 x(x^2+1)^3\\,\\textrm{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "We start by changing the variable:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & ={x}^{2}+1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} & =2x \\\\ \\text{d}u & =2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u & =x\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we create a table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=x^2+1.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & -1 & 0 \\\\ u & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable is\n\n$$\n\\int_{-1}^0 x(x^2+1)^3\\,\\textrm{d}x=\\int_2^1 u^3\\,\\cdot \\dfrac{1}{2}\\textrm{d}u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve it and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{1}{u}^{3}\\,\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{2}^{1}{u}^{3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{{u}^{4}}{4}{|}_{2}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{{1}^{4}-{2}^{4}}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1-16}{4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{15}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Important**: Note that in this example, when we changed the limits the integral $\\displaystyle \\int_{-1}^0 \\ldots \\textrm{d}x$ became $\\displaystyle \\int_{2}^1 \\ldots \\textrm{d}u.$ When changing the limits, it is important to respect the order in which the limits are applied. The lower limit of the integral in $u$ must correspond to the lower limit of the integral in $x.$ Same for the upper limit."} :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Definite Integral of Function Using Substitution"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100877 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100705 :question/id #uuid "dd931400-d592-5402-b380-dee7812adac5" :question/math-academy-id "e-456" :question/problem "Calculate the definite integral $\\displaystyle\\int_{-1}^2 \\dfrac{x^2}{x^3+2}\\,\\textrm{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "We start by changing the variable:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & ={x}^{3}+2 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} & =3{x}^{2} \\\\ \\text{d}u & =3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u & ={x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we create a table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=x^3+2.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & -1 & 2 \\\\ u & 1 & 10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable is\n\n$$\n\\int_{-1}^2 \\dfrac{1}{(x^3+2)}\\,x^2\\textrm{d}x=\\int_1^{10} \\dfrac{1}{u}\\,\\cdot \\dfrac{1}{3}\\textrm{d}u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve it and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{10}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{1}^{10}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(u){|}_{1}^{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Definite Integral of a Rational Function Using Substitution"}] :topic/math-academy-id 1159 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048115 :topic/math-academy-id 758 :topic/title "Improper Integrals"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048150 :topic/math-academy-id 445 :topic/title "Integrating Rational Functions Using Partial Fractions"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048189 :topic/math-academy-id 315 :topic/title "Integration by Substitution With Inverse Trigonometric Functions"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048403 :topic/math-academy-id 316 :topic/title "Further Integration of Algebraic Functions Using Substitution"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050101 :topic/math-academy-id 1112 :topic/title "Integrating Exponential Functions Using Linear Substitution"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050299 :topic/math-academy-id 338 :topic/title "Integrating Functions Using Polynomial Division"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050489 :topic/math-academy-id 478 :topic/title "Integrating Trigonometric Functions Using Substitution"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050898 :topic/math-academy-id 1161 :topic/title "Integrating Logarithmic Functions Using Substitution"}] :topic/title "Calculating Definite Integrals Using Substitution"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048842 :topic/_next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048813 :topic/math-academy-id 1109 :topic/title "The Product Rule for Differentiation"}] :topic/id #uuid "c54eaff0-4e6f-57d2-aa08-401a88379d23" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096620 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096420 :question/id #uuid "932c9b44-c040-5ae1-84f7-277767c255d4" :question/math-academy-id "e-3637" :question/problem "Given that $f(x) = \\dfrac{x} {9x^2 + 5x},$ find $f'(x).$" :question/worked-solution "The numerator and denominator are $u(x) = x$ and $v(x) = 9x^2 + 5x,$ respectively. Their derivatives are given by\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(x) & =1, \\\\ {v}^{′}(x) & =18x+5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the quotient rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t\tf'(x) \t& = \\dfrac {u'(x)\\cdot v(x) - u(x) v'(x)} {[v(x)]^2}\\\n\t\t\t\t& = \\dfrac{\\left( 9x^2+5x \\right )\\cdot 1 -x\\cdot \\left( 18x+5 \\right)}{\\left( 9x^2+5x\\right)^2}\\\n\t\t\t\t& = \\dfrac{\\left( 9x^2+5x-18x^2-5x \\right)}{\\left( 9x^2+5x\\right)^2}\\\n\t\t\t\t& = -\\dfrac{9x^2}{\\left( 9x^2+5x\\right)^2}.\n\t\t}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Quotient of Two Polynomials"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096621 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096421 :question/id #uuid "f804809a-22bf-5ab3-9775-3591d4581797" :question/math-academy-id "e-3638" :question/problem "If $f(x)=\\dfrac{1+\\sin x}{x},$ find $f'(x).$" :question/worked-solution "In this case, the expression for $f(x)$ is a quotient $\\dfrac{u(x)}{v(x)}$ with $u(x) = 1+\\sin x$ and $v(x) = x.$ \n\nTherefore, we apply the quotient rule as follows:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\nf'(x) & = \\dfrac{u'(x)\\cdot v(x) - u(x)\\cdot v'(x)}{[v(x)]^2}\\\n\t\t         \t& = \\dfrac{(1+\\sin x)'\\cdot (x) -(1+\\sin x)\\cdot (x)'}{x^2}\\\n\t\t\t        & = \\dfrac{\\cos x \\cdot x- (1+\\sin x)\\cdot 1}{x^2}\\\n\t\t\t\t& = \\dfrac{x \\cos x -\\sin x -1}{x^2}\t\t\t\n}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Quotient Containing Other Functions"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096622 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096422 :question/id #uuid "53d435ea-a20f-5216-8020-f36cdf8b7c36" :question/math-academy-id "e-3639" :question/problem "Given that $y=\\dfrac{1}{3x+7},$ find $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $u=1$ and $v=3x+7.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} & =0\\,\\text{and}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the quotient rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t\t\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}\t& = \\dfrac{ v\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}-u\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}v}{\\textrm{d}x}}{v^2}\\\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t& = \\dfrac{ \\left( 3x+7 \\right ) \\cdot 0 -1\\cdot 3}{\\left( 3x+7\\right)^2}\\\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t& = -\\dfrac{3}{\\left( 3x+7\\right)^2}.\n\t\t}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Quotient Using Leibniz Notation"}] :topic/math-academy-id 1110 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050961 :topic/math-academy-id 1249 :topic/title "Calculating Derivatives From Data and Tables"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050985 :topic/math-academy-id 1686 :topic/title "Differentiating Reciprocal Trigonometric Functions"}] :topic/title "The Quotient Rule for Differentiation"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049332 :topic/_next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050690 :topic/math-academy-id 1116 :topic/title "Differentiating Logarithmic Functions"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051084 :topic/math-academy-id 1108 :topic/title "The Chain Rule for Differentiation"}] :topic/id #uuid "ec784b83-0c82-5c6a-9dd1-3880cd96d4cb" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092264 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092120 :question/id #uuid "5d2c0fff-c9af-5e8a-846a-640878f08283" :question/math-academy-id "e-730" :question/problem "Find the derivative of $f(x) = \\ln(x^2-4).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$\nf = \\ln u, \\qquad u = x^2-4.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d f}{\\textrm d u} = \\dfrac{1}{u},\\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm d x} = 2x.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{u}\\cdot 2x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-4}\\cdot 2x \\\\  & =\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}-4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Natural Logarithm Function Using the Chain Rule"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092265 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092122 :question/id #uuid "7df7fed3-c172-5cc2-aca8-abd31a0902a0" :question/math-academy-id "e-731" :question/problem "If ![](images/6b/6b4554ae23fd7bb02a3dbeda7d55c342b4f30f07b57133739709048d5bfce44a.png) what is ![](images/1e/1e6f1dc22671990b694ea0386dcdcdd843ea819101a3be99a6c5378d10c5c993.png)" :question/worked-solution "First, we express ![](images/52/523ba398cc130edb20b50aa7b9a5deab0a53e4222824e3deda7f1a13ac7b0a56.png) as the composite function\n\n![](images/7d/7dc97a419c76db6b88c0862e2e54d20062ca6501657bc260eb1c478a5ad1b9eb.png)\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n![](images/c2/c27f0454bd599e68525a774e3e0989a7ee7da4eb82701d6e918ffa334c615916.png)\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n![](images/70/70aaf32dd29ad000b81dcff6e4b155fe6358e923d4d1f485f71aff511233c15b.png)"} :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a General Logarithm Function Using the Chain Rule"}] :topic/math-academy-id 1036 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048191 :topic/math-academy-id 1115 :topic/title "Selecting Procedures for Calculating Derivatives"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048705 :topic/math-academy-id 424 :topic/title "Integration by Parts in Cyclic Cases"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048776 :topic/math-academy-id 1785 :topic/title "Differentiating Inverse Functions"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050400 :topic/math-academy-id 1282 :topic/title "Calculating Derivatives From Data Using the Chain Rule"}] :topic/title "The Chain Rule With Logarithmic Functions"}]}
